                        "content": "     1\u2192# Copyright (c) Meta Platforms, Inc. and affiliates.\n     2\u2192# All rights reserved.\n     3\u2192#\n     4\u2192# This source code is licensed under the BSD-style license found in the\n     5\u2192# LICENSE file in the root directory of this source tree.\n     6\u2192\n     7\u2192import sys\n     8\u2192import time\n     9\u2192\n    10\u2192from functools import partial\n    11\u2192from typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple, Union\n    12\u2192from warnings import warn\n    13\u2192\n    14\u2192import torch\n    15\u2192from omegaconf import DictConfig, ListConfig\n    16\u2192\n    17\u2192from torch import nn\n    18\u2192from torch.distributed import destroy_process_group, init_process_group\n    19\u2192\n    20\u2192from torch.optim import Optimizer\n    21\u2192from torch.utils.data import DataLoader, DistributedSampler\n    22\u2192from torchtune import config, modules, training, utils\n    23\u2192from torchtune.config._utils import _get_component_from_path\n    24\u2192from torchtune.data import padded_collate_packed\n    25\u2192from torchtune.datasets import ConcatDataset\n    26\u2192from torchtune.modules.peft import (\n    27\u2192    DoRALinear,\n    28\u2192    get_adapter_params,\n    29\u2192    get_adapter_state_dict,\n    30\u2192    get_lora_module_names,\n    31\u2192    get_merged_lora_ckpt,\n    32\u2192    load_dora_magnitudes,\n    33\u2192    LoRALinear,\n    34\u2192    set_trainable_params,\n    35\u2192    validate_missing_and_unexpected_for_lora,\n    36\u2192)\n    37\u2192from torchtune.recipe_interfaces import FTRecipeInterface\n    38\u2192from torchtune.training import DummyProfiler, PROFILER_KEY\n    39\u2192\n    40\u2192from tqdm import tqdm\n    41\u2192\n    42\u2192log = utils.get_logger(\"DEBUG\")\n    43\u2192\n    44\u2192\n    45\u2192class LoRAFinetuneRecipeDistributed(FTRecipeInterface):\n    46\u2192    \"\"\"\n    47\u2192    Distributed LoRA finetuning recipe for dense transformer-based LLMs such as Llama2. This recipe supports\n    48\u2192    distributed training and can be run on a single node (1 to 8 GPUs).\n    49\u2192\n    50\u2192    Features:\n    51\u2192        - FSDP. Supported using PyTorch's FSDP APIs. CPU offload of parameters, gradients, and optimizer states\n    52\u2192            is supported via ``fsdp_cpu_offload``. Resharding of parameters after the forward pass is\n    53\u2192            done by default (corresponding to FULL_SHARD sharding strategy), but can be disabled by setting the config\n    54\u2192            ``fsdp_reshard_after_forward`` to False (this corresponds to SHARD_GRAD_OP sharding strategy).\n    55\u2192            DDP is currently not supported. Training on CPU is not supported.\n    56\u2192\n    57\u2192        - Activation Checkpointing. This can be controlled using the ``enable_activation_checkpointing``\n    58\u2192            flag. Activation checkpointing helps reduce the memory footprint since we no longer keep\n    59\u2192            activations in memory and instead recompute them during the backward pass. This is especially\n    60\u2192            helpful for larger batch sizes when you're memory constrained. But these savings in memory\n    61\u2192            come at the cost of training performance. In most cases training can slow-down quite a bit as\n    62\u2192            a result of this activation recomputation.\n    63\u2192\n    64\u2192        - Activation Offloading. This can be controlled using the ``enable_activation_offloading``\n    65\u2192            flag. Activation offloading is a technique similar to activations checkpointing that helps\n    66\u2192            reduce the memory footprint to prevent OOMs on CUDA and enable bigger batches. Where activations\n    67\u2192            checkpointing drops the activation in the forward to recompute it later in the backward,\n    68\u2192            activations offloading will drop the activation in the forward to the CPU and bring it\n    69\u2192            back during the backward pass. As always, there is a tradeoff--these savings in memory can\n    70\u2192            come at the cost of training performance and CPU resources. To recover some runtime cost,\n    71\u2192            we've added an option to enable offloading on a different stream to permit overlapping with\n    72\u2192            the computation. This option is currently only available on PyTorch 2.5.0 or later and will be\n    73\u2192            enabled by default if an acceptable torch version is found. Activation offloading can be used in\n    74\u2192            conjunction with activation checkpointing.\n    75\u2192\n    76\u2192        - Precision. Full fp32 and bf16 training are supported. Precision is controlled using the ``dtype``\n    77\u2192            flag. When ``dtype=bf16``, all activations, gradients and optimizer states are in bfloat16. In\n    78\u2192            most cases this should halve the memory footprint of full precision (fp32) training, without\n    79\u2192            loss in model quality (will depend on the model, training data and other settings). For\n    80\u2192            GPUs which do not support bfloat16, we fall back to fp32. Mixed precision training and fp16\n    81\u2192            precision are currently not supported.\n    82\u2192\n    83\u2192        - Gradient Accumulation. You can simulate larger batch sizes by accumulating gradients. This is\n    84\u2192            controlled using the ``gradient_accumulation_steps`` flag.\n    85\u2192\n    86\u2192                Total Batch Size = batch_size * number of GPUs * gradient accumulation steps.\n    87\u2192\n    88\u2192            For example: with batch_size=1, nproc_per_node=2 and gradient_accumulation_steps=32 we get a\n    89\u2192            total batch size of 64.\n    90\u2192\n    91\u2192            Gradient accumulation is especially useful when you are memory constrained. In this case,\n    92\u2192            accumulating gradients might give you better training speed than enabling activation\n    93\u2192            checkpointing.\n    94\u2192\n    95\u2192        - Checkpointing. Model weights are checkpointed both at the end of each epoch and at the end of\n    96\u2192            training. Currently we checkpoint both the adapter weights (trainable params only) and the\n    97\u2192            complete merged weights (adapter weights added back to the base model). For more details\n    98\u2192            please take a look at our LoRA tutorial\n    99\u2192            (https://pytorch.org/torchtune/main/tutorials/lora_finetune.html).\n   100\u2192\n   101\u2192            Optimizer State and recipe state (seed, total_epochs, number of epochs run etc) are\n   102\u2192            only saved at the end of a given epoch and used in case of resuming training. Resuming\n   103\u2192            training is controlled by the ``resume_from_checkpoint`` flag. Mid-epoch checkpointing is\n   104\u2192            currently not supported.\n   105\u2192\n   106\u2192            For more details on the checkpointer, please take a look at\n   107\u2192            our checkpointer deepdive (https://pytorch.org/torchtune/main/tutorials/checkpointer.html).\n   108\u2192\n   109\u2192        - Logging. Terminal, Disk, WandB and TensorBoard are all supported.\n   110\u2192\n   111\u2192        - Gradient Clipping. Gradient clipping is supported using the ``clip_grad_norm`` flag. By default,\n   112\u2192            ``clip_grad_norm`` is set to ``None``. If you only want to log the grad norm, you can set\n   113\u2192            ``clip_grad_norm='inf'``.\n   114\u2192\n   115\u2192    For a full list of example configs for this recipe, run ``tune ls`` on the command line. Each config\n   116\u2192    has example commands for how to kick-off training.\n   117\u2192\n   118\u2192    Args:\n   119\u2192        cfg (DictConfig): OmegaConf object parsed from yaml file\n   120\u2192\n   121\u2192    Raises:\n   122\u2192        ValueError: If ``dtype`` is set to fp16.\n   123\u2192        ValueError: If world_size is 1\n   124\u2192        RuntimeError: If ``dtype`` is set to bf16 and the hardware does not support bf16.\n   125\u2192        RuntimeError: If ``left_pad_sequence`` is set as the data collator.\n   126\u2192        RuntimeError: If ``enable_activation_offloading`` is True and device is not CUDA.\n   127\u2192        RuntimeError: If ``enable_activation_offloading`` is True and ``enable_activation_checkpointing`` is False.\n   128\u2192    \"\"\"\n   129\u2192\n   130\u2192    def __init__(self, cfg: DictConfig) -> None:\n   131\u2192        self._device = utils.get_device(device=cfg.device)\n   132\u2192        self._dtype = training.get_dtype(cfg.dtype, device=self._device)\n   133\u2192\n   134\u2192        if self._dtype == torch.float16:\n   135\u2192            raise ValueError(\n   136\u2192                \"full fp16 training is not supported with this recipe. Please use bf16 or fp32 instead.\"\n   137\u2192            )\n   138\u2192\n   139\u2192        _, rank = training.get_world_size_and_rank()\n   140\u2192\n   141\u2192        self._is_rank_zero = rank == 0\n   142\u2192\n   143\u2192        # logging attributes\n   144\u2192        self._output_dir = cfg.output_dir\n   145\u2192        self._log_every_n_steps = cfg.get(\"log_every_n_steps\", 1)\n   146\u2192        self._log_peak_memory_stats = cfg.get(\"log_peak_memory_stats\", False)\n   147\u2192\n   148\u2192        if self._log_peak_memory_stats and self._device.type != \"cuda\":\n   149\u2192            log.info(\n   150\u2192                \"log_peak_memory_stats was set to True, however, training does not use cuda. Setting log_peak_memory_stats=False.\"\n   151\u2192            )\n   152\u2192            self._log_peak_memory_stats = False\n   153\u2192\n   154\u2192        # These attributes constitute the recipe state and are updated by ``load_checkpoint``\n   155\u2192        # when ``resume_from_checkpoint`` is ``True``\n   156\u2192        self.seed = training.set_seed(seed=cfg.seed)\n   157\u2192        self.epochs_run = 0\n   158\u2192        self.total_epochs = cfg.epochs\n   159\u2192        self.max_steps_per_epoch = cfg.max_steps_per_epoch\n   160\u2192        self.global_step = 0\n   161\u2192        self._clip_grad_norm = cfg.get(\"clip_grad_norm\", None)\n   162\u2192\n   163\u2192        self._save_adapter_weights_only = cfg.get(\"save_adapter_weights_only\", False)\n   164\u2192        self._resume_from_checkpoint = cfg.resume_from_checkpoint\n   165\u2192        self._gradient_accumulation_steps = cfg.gradient_accumulation_steps\n   166\u2192\n   167\u2192        # activation checkpointing/offloading\n   168\u2192        self._enable_activation_checkpointing = cfg.get(\n   169\u2192            \"enable_activation_checkpointing\", False\n   170\u2192        )\n   171\u2192        self._enable_activation_offloading = cfg.get(\n   172\u2192            \"enable_activation_offloading\", False\n   173\u2192        )\n   174\u2192        if self._enable_activation_offloading:\n   175\u2192            if self._device.type != \"cuda\":\n   176\u2192                raise RuntimeError(\n   177\u2192                    \"enable_activation_offloading should only be True when training on CUDA\"\n   178\u2192                )\n   179\u2192            if not self._enable_activation_checkpointing:\n   180\u2192                raise RuntimeError(\n   181\u2192                    \"enable_activation_offloading should only be True when enable_activation_checkpointing is True\"\n   182\u2192                )\n   183\u2192        elif (\n   184\u2192            self._enable_activation_checkpointing\n   185\u2192            and cfg.checkpointer.model_type != \"LLAMA3_VISION\"\n   186\u2192        ):\n   187\u2192            utils.log_rank_zero(\n   188\u2192                log,\n   189\u2192                \"Hint: enable_activation_checkpointing is True, but enable_activation_offloading isn't. \"\n   190\u2192                \"Enabling activation offloading should reduce memory further.\",\n   191\u2192            )\n   192\u2192\n   193\u2192    def load_checkpoint(self, cfg_checkpointer: DictConfig) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n   194\u2192        \"\"\"\n   195\u2192        Extract the checkpoint state from file and validate. This includes the\n   196\u2192        base model weights. If resume_from_checkpoint is True, this also includes\n   197\u2192        the adapter weights and recipe state\n   198\u2192        \"\"\"\n   199\u2192        self._checkpointer = config.instantiate(\n   200\u2192            cfg_checkpointer,\n   201\u2192            resume_from_checkpoint=self._resume_from_checkpoint,\n   202\u2192        )\n   203\u2192        checkpoint_dict = self._checkpointer.load_checkpoint()\n   204\u2192\n   205\u2192        # When resuming from checkpoint for LoRA, the recipe expects the adapter weights\n   206\u2192        # and recipe state to be present. The keys should match up with what ``save_checkpoint``\n   207\u2192        # used to create these intermediate checkpoints\n   208\u2192        if self._resume_from_checkpoint:\n   209\u2192            if training.ADAPTER_KEY not in checkpoint_dict:\n   210\u2192                raise ValueError(\n   211\u2192                    \"Adapter weights not found. Please ensure a valid adapter checkpoint is provided.\"\n   212\u2192                )\n   213\u2192            # _update_recipe_state will throw an exception if the recipe state is not corrctly loaded\n   214\u2192            # no need to check here\n   215\u2192            self._update_recipe_state(checkpoint_dict)\n   216\u2192        return checkpoint_dict\n   217\u2192\n   218\u2192    def _update_recipe_state(self, ckpt_dict: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n   219\u2192        \"\"\"\n   220\u2192        Updates the recipe state from checkpoint.\n   221\u2192        \"\"\"\n   222\u2192        try:\n   223\u2192            self.epochs_run = ckpt_dict[training.EPOCHS_KEY]\n   224\u2192\n   225\u2192            # on mismatch, warn the user and prevent the override\n   226\u2192            if self.seed != ckpt_dict[training.SEED_KEY]:\n   227\u2192                warn(\n   228\u2192                    message=(\n   229\u2192                        \"Config value for seed does not match the checkpoint value, \"\n   230\u2192                        f\"using the checkpoint value: {ckpt_dict[training.SEED_KEY]}\"\n   231\u2192                    )\n   232\u2192                )\n   233\u2192                self.seed = ckpt_dict[training.SEED_KEY]\n   234\u2192            if self.max_steps_per_epoch != ckpt_dict[training.MAX_STEPS_KEY]:\n   235\u2192                warn(\n   236\u2192                    message=(\n   237\u2192                        \"Config value for max_steps_per_epoch does not match the checkpoint value, \"\n   238\u2192                        f\"using the checkpoint value: {ckpt_dict[training.MAX_STEPS_KEY]}\"\n   239\u2192                    )\n   240\u2192                )\n   241\u2192                self.max_steps_per_epoch = ckpt_dict[training.MAX_STEPS_KEY]\n   242\u2192\n   243\u2192            # on mismatch, warn the user but allow the override\n   244\u2192            if self.total_epochs != ckpt_dict[training.TOTAL_EPOCHS_KEY]:\n   245\u2192                warn(\n   246\u2192                    message=(\n   247\u2192                        \"Config value for total_epochs does not match the checkpoint value, \"\n   248\u2192                        f\"using the config value: {self.total_epochs}\"\n   249\u2192                    )\n   250\u2192                )\n   251\u2192\n   252\u2192        except KeyError as e:\n   253\u2192            raise KeyError(\n   254\u2192                \"Checkpoint does not contain the required keys needed for updating recipe state. \"\n   255\u2192                \"Are you sure you passed in the right recipe checkpoint?\"\n   256\u2192            ) from e\n   257\u2192\n   258\u2192    def setup(self, cfg: DictConfig) -> None:\n   259\u2192        \"\"\"\n   260\u2192        Setup the recipe state. This includes recipe state (if resume_from_checkpoint is True),\n   261\u2192        model, tokenizer, loss, optimizer, learning rate scheduler, sampler, and dataloader.\n   262\u2192        \"\"\"\n   263\u2192        if self._is_rank_zero:\n   264\u2192            self._metric_logger = config.instantiate(cfg.metric_logger)\n   265\u2192\n   266\u2192            # log config with parameter override\n   267\u2192            self._metric_logger.log_config(cfg)\n   268\u2192\n   269\u2192        checkpoint_dict = self.load_checkpoint(cfg_checkpointer=cfg.checkpointer)\n   270\u2192        self._compile = cfg.get(\"compile\", False)\n   271\u2192\n   272\u2192        self._model = self._setup_model(\n   273\u2192            cfg_model=cfg.model,\n   274\u2192            enable_activation_checkpointing=self._enable_activation_checkpointing,\n   275\u2192            enable_activation_offloading=self._enable_activation_offloading,\n   276\u2192            custom_sharded_layers=cfg.get(\"custom_sharded_layers\", None),\n   277\u2192            fsdp_cpu_offload=cfg.get(\"fsdp_cpu_offload\", False),\n   278\u2192            reshard_after_forward=cfg.get(\"fsdp_reshard_after_forward\", True),\n   279\u2192            base_model_state_dict=checkpoint_dict[training.MODEL_KEY],\n   280\u2192            lora_weights_state_dict=(\n   281\u2192                checkpoint_dict[training.ADAPTER_KEY]\n   282\u2192                if self._resume_from_checkpoint\n   283\u2192                else None\n   284\u2192            ),\n   285\u2192        )\n   286\u2192        self._tokenizer = config.instantiate(cfg.tokenizer)\n   287\u2192\n   288\u2192        self._optimizer = self._setup_optimizer(\n   289\u2192            cfg_optimizer=cfg.optimizer,\n   290\u2192            opt_state_dict=(\n   291\u2192                checkpoint_dict[training.OPT_KEY]\n   292\u2192                if self._resume_from_checkpoint\n   293\u2192                else None\n   294\u2192            ),\n   295\u2192        )\n   296\u2192\n   297\u2192        # initialize loss\n   298\u2192        self._loss_fn = config.instantiate(cfg.loss)\n   299\u2192\n   300\u2192        if self._compile:\n   301\u2192            training.compile_loss(self._loss_fn, verbose=self._is_rank_zero)\n   302\u2192\n   303\u2192        if self._loss_fn.__class__.__name__ == \"CEWithChunkedOutputLoss\":\n   304\u2192            # set num_output_chunks for model\n   305\u2192            self._model.set_num_output_chunks(self._loss_fn.num_output_chunks)\n   306\u2192        utils.log_rank_zero(log, \"Loss is initialized.\")\n   307\u2192\n   308\u2192        # sampler and dataloader depend on the tokenizer and loss_fn and should be\n   309\u2192        # setup after all of these are setup\n   310\u2192        collate_name = cfg.get(\"collate_fn\", \"torchtune.data.padded_collate_sft\")\n   311\u2192        self._sampler, self._dataloader = self._setup_data(\n   312\u2192            cfg_dataset=cfg.dataset,\n   313\u2192            shuffle=cfg.shuffle,\n   314\u2192            batch_size=cfg.batch_size,\n   315\u2192            collate_fn=collate_name,\n   316\u2192        )\n   317\u2192\n   318\u2192        # Finally update the recipe state which can only be correctly set after all of the\n   319\u2192        # other components have been initialized and updated.\n   320\u2192\n   321\u2192        # Number of training steps in each epoch depends on the number of batches produced\n   322\u2192        # by the dataloader and the max_steps_per_epoch param set by the user and is used\n   323\u2192        # for logging and tracking training state. This should be computed after the dataloader\n   324\u2192        # has been setup\n   325\u2192        self._steps_per_epoch = (\n   326\u2192            len(self._dataloader) // self._gradient_accumulation_steps\n   327\u2192        )\n   328\u2192        if (\n   329\u2192            self.max_steps_per_epoch is not None\n   330\u2192            and self.max_steps_per_epoch < self._steps_per_epoch\n   331\u2192        ):\n   332\u2192            self._steps_per_epoch = self.max_steps_per_epoch\n   333\u2192        self.global_step = self.epochs_run * self._steps_per_epoch\n   334\u2192\n   335\u2192        # Learning rate scheduler can only be set up after number of steps\n   336\u2192        # has been computed\n   337\u2192        self._lr_scheduler = self._setup_lr_scheduler(\n   338\u2192            cfg_lr_scheduler=cfg.lr_scheduler,\n   339\u2192            num_training_steps=self.total_epochs * self._steps_per_epoch,\n   340\u2192            last_epoch=self.global_step - 1,\n   341\u2192        )\n   342\u2192\n   343\u2192        # Set up profiler, returns DummyProfiler (nullcontext object with no-op `step` method)\n   344\u2192        # if cfg is missing profiler key or if `cfg.profiler.enabled = False`\n   345\u2192        self._profiler = self._setup_profiler(cfg.get(PROFILER_KEY, None))\n   346\u2192\n   347\u2192        # Used to ignore labels for loss computation\n   348\u2192        self.ignore_labels_cache = torch.full(\n   349\u2192            (cfg.batch_size, 1), self._loss_fn.ignore_index, device=self._device\n   350\u2192        )\n   351\u2192\n   352\u2192    def _setup_profiler(\n   353\u2192        self, cfg_profiler: Optional[DictConfig] = None\n   354\u2192    ) -> Union[torch.profiler.profile, DummyProfiler]:\n   355\u2192        \"\"\"\n   356\u2192        Parses the `profiler` section of top-level `cfg` and sets up profiler\n   357\u2192\n   358\u2192        Args:\n   359\u2192            cfg_profiler (Optional[DictConfig]): ``profiler`` section of the top-level ``cfg`` (the main config passed to\n   360\u2192                `recipe.main`). Default None.\n   361\u2192\n   362\u2192        Returns:\n   363\u2192            profiler: Union[torch.profiler.profile, DummyProfiler] - DummyProfiler is a nullcontext with no-op methods\n   364\u2192            for `start`, `stop`, and `step` that can be used in place of `torch.profiler.profile` if profiler is not enabled such\n   365\u2192            that the instrumented training loop does not need to be changed profiling is disabled.\n   366\u2192\n   367\u2192        The profiler config can be provided in configs under the `profiler` key with the following layout:\n   368\u2192\n   369\u2192        .. code-block:: yaml\n   370\u2192            profiler:\n   371\u2192                enabled: bool\n   372\u2192\n   373\u2192                #Output directory of trace artifacts\n   374\u2192                output_dir: str\n   375\u2192\n   376\u2192            #`torch.profiler.ProfilerActivity` types to trace\n   377\u2192            cpu: bool\n   378\u2192            cuda: bool\n   379\u2192\n   380\u2192                #Trace options\n   381\u2192                profile_memory: bool\n   382\u2192                with_stack: bool\n   383\u2192                record_shapes: bool\n   384\u2192                with_flops: bool\n   385\u2192\n   386\u2192            # `torch.profiler.schedule` options:\n   387\u2192            # wait_steps -> wait, warmup_steps -> warmup, active_steps -> active, num_cycles -> repeat\n   388\u2192            wait_steps: int\n   389\u2192            warmup_steps: int\n   390\u2192            active_steps: int\n   391\u2192            num_cycles: int\n   392\u2192        \"\"\"\n   393\u2192        # Missing profiler section in config, assume disabled\n   394\u2192        if cfg_profiler is None:\n   395\u2192            cfg_profiler = DictConfig({\"enabled\": False})\n   396\u2192\n   397\u2192        # Check that component is included and set correctly\n   398\u2192        if cfg_profiler.get(\"_component_\", None) is None:\n   399\u